course: dictionary
lesson: 6786
title: Words for great speaking
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Oratory is the skill of giving powerful, good speeches in public. Look at the clues in this sentence: «the long continuous drone of the Badger's voice, rising and falling in waves of oratory». A drone is a dull, steady hum, so the Badger keeps talking for a long time, and the waves show his voice going up and down. Try the meaning: "the Badger's speech-making" fits the sentence, so oratory means speech-making here.
explain: The clues go on. Toad has «long-drawn sobs» and is «very easily converted to any point of view». The speech moved Toad so much that he agreed with whatever he had just heard. Strong oratory can do that to listeners. Always check: swap your meaning in and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Oratory has a second meaning, used in church. An oratory is a small chapel or room set aside for private prayer, and the London Oratory is a particular Catholic church and community. In that sense the word is about prayer, not speaking. The words around the word always tell you which meaning the writer means.
fact: Oratory means the skill of giving powerful, good speeches in public.
fact: Oratory is uncountable when it means the skill: you say good oratory, not a good oratory.
fact: Martin Luther King Jr. is famous for his oratory, including the speech with the words «I have a dream».
fact: In church life, an oratory (plural: oratories) is a small chapel or room for private prayer.
fact: The London Oratory is a Catholic church and community in London.
exercise: In «the Badger's voice, rising and falling in waves of oratory», what does oratory mean? | *Speech-making, the skill of speaking to an audience | A small chapel for private prayer | A loud song | A quiet whisper
exercise: Which famous speaker is known for the words «I have a dream»? | A small chapel | *Martin Luther King Jr. | A petrol station | Toad
exercise: In church life, what is an oratory? | *A small chapel or room for private prayer | A type of bird | A loud speech given in a park | A tall tower of bells
test: Toad was «very easily converted to any point of view». What does this tell you about the speech? | It was given in a church | *It was powerful enough to change Toad's mind | It was dull and nobody listened | It was too short to matter
test: In a debating club, what is the best meaning of oratory? | Drawing pictures | Running races | Quiet reading in a library | *Skilful, powerful speaking in public
exam: What is the best way to check that oratory means speech-making in the sentence? | *Put speech-making in place of oratory and see if the sentence still makes sense | Guess, because the word is too long to understand | Look only at the first word of the sentence | Skip the word and never come back to it
